# function fib(n) { if (n < 2) return n; return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2); }
# function down(n) { if (n < 1) return 0; return down(n - 1); }
# A function that calls itself is a loop written as calls. Where the unit walks its own body a written call of itself is copied in by the rule of calls that stand in loops: half of fib's calls are leaves, and they are gone.
# Only the unit's own body copies: in a copy the calls of itself stay calls, so there is one level and no more.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: frame 1 callee=f1
# CHECK: frame 2 callee=f1
# CHECK: = CallDirect {{*}}function=f1
# CHECK: = CallDirect {{*}}function=f1
# CHECK: = CallDirect {{*}}function=f1
# CHECK: = CallDirect {{*}}function=f1
# RUN(ONCE): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# ONCE-NOT: frame 3
# A call of itself whose answer is returned at once is the back edge of the loop: a copy would write the body twice and leave the call.
# RUN(BACK): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# BACK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# BACK-NOT: frame 1
# RUN(WITHOUT): walk f1 mechanisms=all-Inlining
# WITHOUT-NOT: frame 1

